VALUES (
  'issue-001-state-of-local-ai-sept-2026',
  'Welcome to the Dispatch — State of Local AI, September 2026',
  'What''s actually worth running locally in 2026, hardware picks by budget, and why the gap between local and cloud is closing faster than anyone expected.',
  E'# Welcome to the Aethyro Dispatch\n\nEvery week or two I sit down and filter the noise. Local AI moves fast — models drop, hardware gets cheaper, hype cycles spin. This newsletter is about what actually matters if you want intelligence on your own machine, under your own control.\n\nLet''s get into it.\n\n---\n\n## State of Local AI: September 2026\n\nThree years ago, running a useful language model locally meant a water-cooled gaming rig and a lot of patience. Today it means an M3 MacBook Air on your lap.\n\nHere''s the honest landscape right now:\n\n**7B models are genuinely useful.** Not just for toy demos — for writing, summarising, coding drafts, and Q&A over your own documents. Llama 3.2 7B, Mistral 7B v0.3, and Qwen 3.5 7B all cross the "I would actually use this" threshold. Quantised to Q4_K_M they run comfortably on 8 GB of unified memory.\n\n**32B is the sweet spot.** If your machine has 24–32 GB of RAM or VRAM, the 32B class is where the quality jump becomes visceral. Reasoning improves, instruction-following tightens, and outputs start feeling like a sharp junior colleague rather than an autocomplete engine.\n\n**Apple Silicon keeps compounding.** The M-series unified memory architecture is a structural advantage for local AI. An M4 Max with 48 GB runs a 32B model at ~35 tokens/second — faster than you read. That speed matters: it changes the interaction from "waiting for the model" to "actually having a conversation."\n\n**The cloud gap is real but narrowing.** GPT-4o and Claude Opus are still better at complex multi-step reasoning. But for the 80% of daily tasks — drafting, explaining, summarising, first-draft code — the best local 32B models are close enough that the privacy and cost arguments win easily.\n\n---\n\n## Hardware Tiers — What to Run Where\n\n| Budget | Hardware | Model Sweet Spot | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Free (you own it) | 8 GB unified RAM (M1/M2 Air) | Qwen 3.5 7B Q4_K_M | ~25 tok/s |\n| Mid | 16 GB unified RAM (M3 Pro) | Mistral 32B Q4_K_M | ~18 tok/s |\n| Power | 32 GB unified RAM (M4 Max / M3 Ultra) | Qwen 3.5 32B Q5_K_M | ~30 tok/s |\n| Enthusiast | RTX 5090 (24 GB VRAM) | 70B Q3_K_M | ~22 tok/s |\n\n*Token speeds are approximate real-world generation rates, not benchmark peaks.*\n\nIf you''re buying hardware specifically for local AI, **M4 MacBook Pro 24 GB is the current best all-rounder** — fast, silent, great battery, and the memory bandwidth (273 GB/s) outperforms a 3090 for most LLM workloads.\n\n---\n\n## Model Pick of the Week: Qwen 3.5 32B\n\nQwen 3.5 32B is the model I keep coming back to this month. Alibaba''s latest iteration lands in an interesting spot: it genuinely rivals GPT-4o-mini on instruction following, beats it on Chinese and multilingual tasks, and costs you $0 to run.\n\nThinking mode (activate with `/think` in the prompt or set the system prompt flag) adds a chain-of-thought reasoning step that lifts performance on hard math and logic problems by a meaningful margin.\n\n**Get it running in 60 seconds:**\n\n```bash\n# Pull and run with Ollama\nollama pull qwen3.5:32b\nollama run qwen3.5:32b\n```\n\n**Or point Aethyro at your local instance:**\n1. Start Ollama: `OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 ollama serve`\n2. In Aethyro → Settings → Custom endpoint → `http://localhost:11434`\n3. Select `qwen3.5:32b` from the model picker\n\nYou''re now running a frontier-class model locally, privately, for free.\n\n---\n\n## Build Guide: Context Windows vs. Speed — Know the Tradeoff\n\nEvery model has a context window — the maximum text it can "see" at once. Qwen 3.5 32B supports 128k tokens. That sounds like a superpower. Here''s the catch:\n\n**Prefill is expensive.** Feeding 100k tokens into the model before generation starts costs time and memory. On a 32 GB machine, a 100k context fill for a 32B model takes ~8 seconds. A 4k context takes ~0.3 seconds.\n\n**Practical rules:**\n- **Under 8k tokens** — use freely, no noticeable lag.\n- **8k–32k** — fine for document Q&A, code review of a whole file.\n- **32k–128k** — reserve for genuine need (full codebases, long transcripts). Expect 5–15 second prefill.\n- **Beyond 128k** — not all Ollama builds handle this gracefully yet. Test before relying on it.\n\nFor most chat tasks, keeping context tight (trim old messages, use `/clear` between topics) gives you faster, better-quality responses. The model''s "attention" is finite — a clean context often beats a stuffed one.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Hits\n\n- **Ollama 0.9.1** dropped with improved memory management on Apple Silicon — update if you haven''t.\n- **LM Studio 0.3** adds a local server mode that makes it easy to point any OpenAI-compatible client at your machine.\n- **llama.cpp** merged speculative decoding for GGUF models — up to 2× speedup on CPU-only setups.\n- **Privacy reminder:** When you run models locally through Aethyro, your messages never leave your machine. The inference happens on your hardware. That''s the whole point.\n\n---\n\n## Next Issue\n\nWe''re going deep on **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** — how to attach your own documents, notes, and data to a local model so it can actually answer questions about your life and work. I''ll cover the tools, the tradeoffs, and a working setup you can build in an afternoon.\n\nIf you have a question you want answered in the newsletter, reply to this email or tap "Discuss with Aethyro" on any issue.\n\n— The Aethyro Team',
  false,
  '2026-09-22 09:00:00+00'
)
